---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';

const pageSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "TechArticle",
  "headline": "Spain Digital Nomad Visa & Beckham Law: Flat 24% Tax Rate Explained (2026)",
  "description": "How remote tech workers and digital nomads access Spain's flat 24% Beckham Law tax regime. Minimum income requirements, social security exemptions, and tax savings math.",
  "datePublished": "2026-09-02T00:00:00Z",
  "dateModified": "2026-09-14T00:00:00Z",
  "author": {
    "@type": "Organization",
    "name": "NomadTreaty Intelligence"
  }
};

const faqSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": [
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What is the exact tax rate under Spain's Beckham Law for digital nomads?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Under the Beckham Law (Special Impatriate Regime), remote workers pay a flat 24% tax rate on all Spanish-source earned income up to €600,000 per year. Earnings exceeding €600,000 are taxed at 47%."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "What is the filing deadline for Modelo 149 to elect the Beckham Law?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "You must formally submit Modelo 149 to the Agencia Tributaria within exactly six months from the date of registration with the Spanish Social Security system (or equivalent consular notification date)."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Are foreign dividends and capital gains taxed under the Beckham Law?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "No. Under Article 93 of the Spanish Personal Income Tax Act, non-Spanish source investment dividends, interest, and capital gains are 100% exempt from Spanish taxation and excluded from Modelo 720 asset reporting."
      }
    }
  ]
};

const breadcrumbSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
  "itemListElement": [
    {
      "@type": "ListItem",
      "position": 1,
      "name": "Home",
      "item": "https://nomadtreaty.vercel.app/"
    },
    {
      "@type": "ListItem",
      "position": 2,
      "name": "Spain Beckham Law Guide",
      "item": "https://nomadtreaty.vercel.app/spain-digital-nomad-visa-beckham-law-guide/"
    }
  ]
};

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [pageSchema, faqSchema, breadcrumbSchema]
};
---

<Layout
  title="Spain Beckham Law 2026: 24% Flat Tax Sizer & Guide"
  description="How to qualify for Spain's 24% flat tax rate under the Beckham Law as a remote worker or contractor. Detailed income thresholds, paperwork checklist, and net salary math."
  slug="spain-digital-nomad-visa-beckham-law-guide"
  schema={schema}
>
  <article class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 py-12">
    <!-- Breadcrumb Nav -->
    <nav class="flex items-center gap-2 text-xs text-slate-400 mb-6 font-medium">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-emerald-400 transition-colors">Home</a>
      <span>/</span>
      <span class="text-emerald-400">Spain Beckham Law Guide</span>
    </nav>

    <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-3 py-1 rounded-full bg-emerald-500/10 border border-emerald-500/20 text-emerald-400 text-xs font-semibold mb-4">
      Spanish Startup Act (Ley de Startups) • Comprehensive 2026 Audit Edition
    </div>

    <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl lg:text-5xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-white mb-6 leading-tight">
      Spain Digital Nomad Visa & Beckham Law: Flat 24% Tax Rate Explained (2026)
    </h1>

    <!-- Quick Answer Box -->
    <div class="bg-emerald-950/40 border border-emerald-500/30 rounded-2xl p-6 mb-8 backdrop-blur-sm shadow-xl">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-2 text-emerald-400 font-bold text-xs uppercase tracking-wider mb-2">
        <span>⚡</span> Quick Answer (The Spanish Tax Formula)
      </div>
      <p class="text-slate-200 text-base leading-relaxed font-medium">
        Under Spain's Startup Act, holders of the Digital Nomad Visa qualify for the <strong>Beckham Law (Special Expat Tax Regime)</strong>, capping personal income tax at a <strong>flat 24% up to €600,000</strong> for up to 6 consecutive tax years, compared to normal progressive brackets reaching 47%.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Comparative Tax Savings Table -->
    <div class="my-8 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-slate-800 bg-slate-900/60 p-1 shadow-2xl">
      <table class="w-full text-left text-sm text-slate-300">
        <thead class="bg-slate-900 text-xs uppercase tracking-wider text-slate-400 border-b border-slate-800">
          <tr>
            <th class="px-5 py-3.5 font-bold text-white">Gross Annual Income</th>
            <th class="px-5 py-3.5 font-bold text-rose-400">Standard Progressive IRPF (Madrid/Catalonia)</th>
            <th class="px-5 py-3.5 font-bold text-emerald-400">Beckham Law (Flat 24%)</th>
            <th class="px-5 py-3.5 font-bold text-cyan-300">Annual Net Cash Advantage</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-800 text-xs font-mono">
          <tr>
            <td class="px-5 py-3 font-semibold text-white">€45,000</td>
            <td class="px-5 py-3 text-rose-300">€10,850 (24.1%)</td>
            <td class="px-5 py-3 text-emerald-400 font-bold">€10,800 (24.0%)</td>
            <td class="px-5 py-3 text-cyan-300 font-bold">+€50 / yr (Break-even tier)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="px-5 py-3 font-semibold text-white">€65,000</td>
            <td class="px-5 py-3 text-rose-300">€19,450 (29.9%)</td>
            <td class="px-5 py-3 text-emerald-400 font-bold">€15,600 (24.0%)</td>
            <td class="px-5 py-3 text-cyan-300 font-bold">+€3,850 / yr</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="px-5 py-3 font-semibold text-white">€95,000</td>
            <td class="px-5 py-3 text-rose-300">€33,950 (35.7%)</td>
            <td class="px-5 py-3 text-emerald-400 font-bold">€22,800 (24.0%)</td>
            <td class="px-5 py-3 text-cyan-300 font-bold">+€11,150 / yr</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="px-5 py-3 font-semibold text-white">€140,000</td>
            <td class="px-5 py-3 text-rose-300">€56,200 (40.1%)</td>
            <td class="px-5 py-3 text-emerald-400 font-bold">€33,600 (24.0%)</td>
            <td class="px-5 py-3 text-cyan-300 font-bold">+€22,600 / yr</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="px-5 py-3 font-semibold text-white">€220,000</td>
            <td class="px-5 py-3 text-rose-300">€95,400 (43.4%)</td>
            <td class="px-5 py-3 text-emerald-400 font-bold">€52,800 (24.0%)</td>
            <td class="px-5 py-3 text-cyan-300 font-bold">+€42,600 / yr</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="px-5 py-3 font-semibold text-white">€500,000</td>
            <td class="px-5 py-3 text-rose-300">€228,500 (45.7%)</td>
            <td class="px-5 py-3 text-emerald-400 font-bold">€120,000 (24.0%)</td>
            <td class="px-5 py-3 text-cyan-300 font-bold">+€108,500 / yr</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <!-- Deep Technical Content -->
    <div class="prose prose-invert max-w-none text-slate-300 space-y-8 text-sm sm:text-base leading-relaxed">
      
      <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-white tracking-tight pt-4">
        1. Beckham Law vs Standard Spanish IRPF: Marginal Bracket Calculus
      </h2>
      <p>
        The Spanish personal income tax system (<em>Impuesto sobre la Renta de las Personas Físicas</em> or IRPF) is split into dual state and autonomous regional brackets. Under standard resident taxation in tech hubs like Barcelona (Catalonia) or Madrid, effective rates climb aggressively from 19% on the first €12,450 to 37% at €35,200, hitting top marginal tiers of <strong>45% to 47%</strong> on earnings over €60,000 and €300,000 respectively.
      </p>
      <p>
        Enacted under Article 93 of the Spanish Personal Income Tax Act and updated via the 2023–2026 Startup Act reforms, the <strong>Special Impatriate Regime</strong> (colloquially known as the Beckham Law) fundamentally decouples qualifying international remote professionals from progressive tax bands:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 text-sm text-slate-300 ml-2">
        <li><strong>Fixed Flat Rate:</strong> A non-progressive 24% rate applies to all employment income derived up to €600,000.</li>
        <li><strong>Surtax Ceiling:</strong> Any dollar or euro earned above €600,000 is taxed at a flat 47%, eliminating regional deviations.</li>
        <li><strong>Duration of Benefit:</strong> The exemption applies during the fiscal tax year of relocation plus the subsequent <strong>5 full consecutive calendar tax years</strong> (totaling up to 6 taxable years of protection).</li>
        <li><strong>Break-Even Economic Sizing:</strong> For remote employees earning under €45,000 gross, standard IRPF with family personal deductions may yield parity. However, once gross billings surpass <strong>€60,000</strong>, the Beckham Law generates net annual cash savings ranging from €3,500 to over €100,000 per year.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-white tracking-tight pt-4">
        2. Statutory Eligibility Criteria: The 5-Year Prior Non-Residency Rule
      </h2>
      <p>
        Historically, obtaining Beckham Law clearance required non-tax residence in Spain for ten preceding years. The revised regulatory framework has drastically lowered this friction:
      </p>
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 my-6 font-mono text-xs">
        <div class="p-5 rounded-xl bg-slate-900 border border-slate-800">
          <div class="text-emerald-400 font-bold mb-2 uppercase">5-Year Prior Non-Residency</div>
          <p class="text-slate-300 font-sans text-xs leading-relaxed">
            The candidate must not have maintained primary tax residence or spent more than 183 days in Spanish territory during the <strong>5 calendar years</strong> immediately preceding their relocation year.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="p-5 rounded-xl bg-slate-900 border border-slate-800">
          <div class="text-emerald-400 font-bold mb-2 uppercase">80% Foreign Income Requirement</div>
          <p class="text-slate-300 font-sans text-xs leading-relaxed">
            If applying as a qualified remote worker or contractor, a minimum of <strong>80% of professional revenue</strong> must originate from corporate entities located outside the Kingdom of Spain.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>
        Eligible applicants fall into three well-defined statutory classes: (1) Remote employees relocating with a foreign employment agreement, (2) Highly qualified entrepreneurs and tech executives operating innovative startups certified by ENISA, and (3) Freelance contractors possessing a relevant university bachelor's degree or minimum 3 years of verifiable professional technology experience.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-white tracking-tight pt-4">
        3. Social Security Bilateral Agreements & Totalization Treaties
      </h2>
      <p>
        One of the most dangerous hidden costs in Spanish international relocation is mandatory social security. Standard Spanish autonomous freelancers (<em>Autónomos</em>) are enrolled in RETA (<em>Régimen Especial de Trabajadores Autónomos</em>), requiring mandatory tiered social contributions ranging from €230 to €590 per month regardless of billing status.
      </p>
      <p>
        Digital nomads can legally eliminate or redirect this liability through bilateral totalization agreements:
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 text-sm text-slate-300 ml-2">
        <li><strong>United States Remote Workers:</strong> Under the US-Spain Social Security Totalization Treaty, American remote employees maintaining an active W-2 payroll relationship can submit Form <strong>USA/E-1 (Certificate of Coverage)</strong> from the US Social Security Administration. This grants legal exemption from Spanish social security contributions for up to 5 full years.</li>
        <li><strong>United Kingdom & European Union Nomads:</strong> Utilizing <strong>A1 Portable Documents</strong> under EU Regulation 883/2004 or the UK-EU Trade and Cooperation Agreement, EU/UK citizens remain insured under their home country healthcare and pension regimes, bypassing Spanish national healthcare payroll taxes completely.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-white tracking-tight pt-4">
        4. Step-by-Step Filing Protocol: Modelo 149 & 6-Month Hard Deadlines
      </h2>
      <p>
        The Beckham Law is <strong>never automatically applied</strong> upon receiving the Digital Nomad Visa stamp or landing in Madrid-Barajas airport. It requires an explicit election filed with the <em>Agencia Estatal de Administración Tributaria</em> (AEAT):
      </p>
      <div class="p-6 rounded-xl bg-slate-900/90 border border-slate-800 shadow-xl my-4">
        <h3 class="text-white font-bold text-base mb-3 flex items-center gap-2">
          <span class="text-amber-400">⚠️</span> The Strict 6-Month Statutory Window
        </h3>
        <p class="text-xs text-slate-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          The election must be filed via <strong>Modelo 149</strong> within exactly <strong>6 months</strong> from the date of registration in the Spanish Social Security system, or the date of the formal start of remote activity indicated on your consular certificate. Failing to lodge Modelo 149 within this strict calendar window permanently disqualifies the applicant, automatically pushing their entire worldwide income into the standard progressive IRPF schedule.
        </p>
        <div class="text-xs font-mono space-y-2 text-slate-300 bg-slate-950 p-4 rounded-lg border border-slate-800">
          <div>Step 1: Obtain NIE (Número de Identificación de Extranjero) & Cl@ve PIN digital certificate.</div>
          <div>Step 2: Procure certified Certificate of Coverage or Spanish Social Security affiliation number.</div>
          <div>Step 3: Secure employer declaration confirming purely remote teleworking capability from Spain.</div>
          <div>Step 4: Electronically submit Modelo 149 via the AEAT Sede Electrónica.</div>
          <div>Step 5: Receive formal AEAT approval resolution certificate (Resolución de Adscripción).</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-white tracking-tight pt-4">
        5. Wealth Tax, Modelo 720 & Capital Gains Shielding Under Article 93
      </h2>
      <p>
        Perhaps the single greatest financial multiplier of the Beckham Law for founders and software engineers is its comprehensive territorial isolation of non-Spanish capital assets:
      </p>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-6 rounded-xl border border-slate-800 bg-slate-900/60 p-1">
        <table class="w-full text-left text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-300">
          <thead class="bg-slate-900 text-xs font-mono uppercase text-slate-400 border-b border-slate-800">
            <tr>
              <th class="p-3 font-bold text-white">Asset / Tax Dimension</th>
              <th class="p-3 font-bold text-rose-400">Standard Spanish Tax Resident</th>
              <th class="p-3 font-bold text-emerald-400">Beckham Law Article 93 Expat</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-800 text-xs font-mono">
            <tr>
              <td class="p-3 text-white font-sans font-semibold">Foreign Stock Portfolio Gains</td>
              <td class="p-3 text-rose-300">Taxed at 19% to 28%</td>
              <td class="p-3 text-emerald-400 font-bold">0% Spanish Tax (Territorial Exemption)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="p-3 text-white font-sans font-semibold">Foreign Dividends & Interest</td>
              <td class="p-3 text-rose-300">Taxed at savings rates up to 28%</td>
              <td class="p-3 text-emerald-400 font-bold">0% Spanish Tax</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="p-3 text-white font-sans font-semibold">Modelo 720 Worldwide Asset Report</td>
              <td class="p-3 text-rose-300">Mandatory disclosure of all foreign banks/crypto</td>
              <td class="p-3 text-emerald-400 font-bold">EXEMPT (Zero Disclosure Required)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="p-3 text-white font-sans font-semibold">Spanish Wealth Tax (Impuesto Patrimonio)</td>
              <td class="p-3 text-rose-300">Levied on worldwide net assets (up to 3.5%)</td>
              <td class="p-3 text-emerald-400 font-bold">Levied ONLY on Spanish real estate/bank assets</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        Because Beckham Law beneficiaries are treated fiscally as non-residents under the Non-Resident Income Tax Act (IRNR), you pay taxes <strong>exclusively on Spanish-source income</strong>. If you sell equity in a Delaware C-Corp, realize crypto capital gains on foreign exchanges, or earn dividends from global index funds, Spain takes exactly €0.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-white tracking-tight pt-4">
        6. Frequently Asked Questions: Spanish Expat Tax Optimization
      </h2>
      <div class="space-y-4 my-6">
        <div class="p-5 rounded-xl bg-slate-900 border border-slate-800">
          <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-white mb-2">Can family members and spouses also benefit from the Beckham Law?</h3>
          <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
            Yes. Under the Startup Act reforms, accompanying spouses and dependent children under the age of 25 can also access the flat 24% tax rate under their primary applicant's certificate, provided they relocate within the first year of the visa holder's residency.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="p-5 rounded-xl bg-slate-900 border border-slate-800">
          <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-white mb-2">What happens after the 6-year Beckham Law period expires?</h3>
          <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
            In year 7, if you choose to maintain your permanent residency in Spain, you transition automatically into the standard progressive IRPF tax tables and become subject to worldwide asset reporting (Modelo 720) and standard capital gains taxation.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="p-5 rounded-xl bg-slate-900 border border-slate-800">
          <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-white mb-2">What is the minimum monthly income requirement for the Digital Nomad Visa in 2026?</h3>
          <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
            The financial threshold is pegged at 200% of the Spanish minimum wage (Salario Mínimo Interprofesional / SMI), which requires proof of at least €2,670 per month (approx. $2,950 USD) in verifiable recurring remote income over the prior 3 months.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

    </div>

    <!-- Related Links -->
    <div class="mt-12 pt-8 border-t border-slate-900 flex flex-col sm:flex-row justify-between gap-4 text-xs font-mono">
      <a href="/" class="text-emerald-400 hover:underline">← Back to Global Tax Treaty Matrix</a>
      <a href="/portugal-nhr-tax-nomad-calculator-2026/" class="text-emerald-400 hover:underline">Compare Portugal NHR 2.0 Tax Scheme →</a>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>
